<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pomaks</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pomaks are Bulgarian-speaking Muslims living mainly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodope-mountains">Rhodope Mountains</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and in neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. They are descended from Slavic Christians who converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> under Ottoman rule, mostly between the 16th and 18th centuries. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877–1878 Pomak villages in the western Rhodopes resisted Russian control and formed the self-governing Republic of Tamrash.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pomaks.jpg" alt="Pomaks" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pomak_Photos_0006.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
